# Daymark Companion — example environment.
# Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and adjust. Compose reads variables referenced as ${VAR}
# automatically. Names match docs/COMPANION_DEPLOYMENT.md.
#
# This stack has NO bundled reverse proxy. It runs the application on 127.0.0.1:8080 and
# expects your own proxy — Cosmos Cloud, Caddy, Traefik, nginx, a Cloudflare tunnel — to
# terminate TLS in front of it. Worked configs live in docs/alternatives/.

# --- Required: no default, on purpose ---------------------------------------
# docker-compose.yml references this with the `${VAR:?}` form, so a missing value fails the
# deploy loudly instead of silently starting on the wrong hostname.
#
# The public hostname clients reach, as they type it. This is the WebAuthn RP-ID and the
# base URL used to build invite links in outbound email, so it must be the real external
# origin — not a LAN alias, not the container name. Get it wrong and passkeys silently
# refuse to work and invite links point somewhere nobody can reach.
DAYMARK_DOMAIN=daymark.example.com

# Scheme of that origin. Leave as https unless you are on a trusted LAN with no TLS —
# in which case WebAuthn will not work at all, browsers require a secure context.
# DAYMARK_PUBLIC_SCHEME=https

# --- Networking / serving ---
DAYMARK_BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0
DAYMARK_PORT=8080
DAYMARK_BASE_PATH=/            # set to e.g. /daymark to serve under a sub-path
DAYMARK_DATA_DIR=/data
DAYMARK_LOG_LEVEL=warn         # info is chattier; warn is the deployment default

# Where the container's port is published on the host. Loopback only by default: your
# proxy runs on this host and forwards to it, and nothing on the LAN can reach it.
# DAYMARK_HOST_PORT=8080
#
# Only change DAYMARK_BIND_IP if your proxy is on ANOTHER machine and the link between
# them is already encrypted (VPN, WireGuard, private VLAN). The app speaks plain HTTP and
# authenticates with a bearer token: anything that can read the wire can replay it.
# DAYMARK_BIND_IP=127.0.0.1

# --- How your proxy reaches the app -----------------------------------------
# a. Proxy on this HOST (nginx, Caddy, Cosmos in host-network mode)
#      Point it at http://127.0.0.1:8080. That is the default; nothing else to do.
#
# b. Proxy in a CONTAINER (Cosmos Cloud's normal mode, Traefik, Nginx Proxy Manager)
#      docker network connect daymark-companion_back <your-proxy-container>
#      upstream: http://daymark-companion:8080
#    Then switch to the no-egress override, which removes the published host port and
#    leaves the app with no route off its network at all:
#      docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.no-egress.yml up -d
#
# EITHER WAY, add Strict-Transport-Security at your proxy. The app sends CSP,
# X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options and Referrer-Policy on every response, but
# deliberately not HSTS, because it cannot know whether it is behind TLS.

# --- Reverse proxy: the trusted-proxy contract ------------------------------
# Default is TRUST NOTHING, which is why this line is empty. Set it to the proxy's exact
# source IP/CIDR so X-Forwarded-* is honoured ONLY from that proxy. Never use a broad range
# like 172.16.0.0/12 — any co-resident container could then spoof headers and walk past the
# auth lockout.
#
# This is NOT cosmetic. Leave it unset behind a proxy and every per-client control —
# sync auth lockout, TOTP lockout, the recovery rate limit — keys on the proxy's address,
# so all clients share one bucket and eight bad tokens from one attacker lock out
# everybody.
#
# Set it to the proxy's address AS THE APP SEES IT, which is not always the address you
# think:
#   - proxy on the host, forwarding to the published loopback port: usually the docker0
#     gateway (commonly 172.17.0.1), NOT the proxy's LAN address
#   - proxy in a container attached to daymark-companion_back: that container's address on
#     that network (allocated from 10.89.0.0/24; the app itself is pinned at 10.89.0.3)
# `docker inspect` the proxy container, or read the address out of the warning below.
#
# Getting it wrong FAILS QUIET: forwarded headers are ignored and everyone shares one
# lockout bucket, exactly as if it were unset — there is no error, because "ignore
# untrusted headers" is also the correct behaviour. To make that visible, the server logs
# a one-time WARN naming the address it actually saw, the first time an X-Forwarded-For
# arrives while this is empty:
#
#   docker compose logs companion | grep X-Forwarded-For
#
# If lockouts start affecting users who did nothing, suspect this line.
DAYMARK_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# --- Public origin overrides (normally derived from DAYMARK_DOMAIN) ----------
# Compose builds all three from DAYMARK_DOMAIN + DAYMARK_PUBLIC_SCHEME. Override in
# docker-compose.override.yml only if your RP-ID must differ from the host (e.g. passkeys
# registered against a parent domain).
#   DAYMARK_PUBLIC_BASE_URL   https://daymark.example.com
#   DAYMARK_WEBAUTHN_RP_ID    daymark.example.com
#   DAYMARK_WEBAUTHN_ORIGINS  https://daymark.example.com

# --- Outbound SMTP (the ONE deliberate egress exception; OFF by default) ---
# Enabled ONLY when DAYMARK_SMTP_HOST is set. Egresses to this one host:port, requires
# TLS, and its emails carry ONLY therapist invite/notification LINKS — never any record
# or plaintext content.
#
# The default network disables NAT masquerading, so nothing outbound gets a reply. Turning
# SMTP on therefore also means restoring an egress path: see §2.1 of
# docs/COMPANION_DEPLOYMENT_HARDENING.md. It is NOT compatible with the no-egress override
# at all — that network has no gateway by design.
# Password should come from a *_FILE secret.
# DAYMARK_SMTP_HOST=mail.example.org
# DAYMARK_SMTP_PORT=587                 # 587 STARTTLS, or 465 with implicit TLS
# DAYMARK_SMTP_TLS=starttls             # starttls | implicit (never 'none' — refused)
# DAYMARK_SMTP_FROM=companion@example.org
# DAYMARK_SMTP_USER=companion           # optional; omit for unauthenticated submission
# DAYMARK_SMTP_PASS_FILE=/run/secrets/companion_smtp_pass

# --- Limits (Config.kt defaults; compose states them explicitly so they are auditable) ---
# DAYMARK_MAX_BLOB_BYTES=26214400
# DAYMARK_MAX_VERSIONS=200
# DAYMARK_RATE_LIMIT_RPS=5

# --- The bearer token is NOT set here ---------------------------------------
# It is a file-backed docker secret: an env var is readable via `docker inspect` and
# leaks into shell history and process listings. Create it before the first start:
#
#   mkdir -p secrets && chmod 700 secrets
#   openssl rand -base64 48 | tr -d '\n' > secrets/auth_token
#   sudo chown 65532:65532 secrets/auth_token && chmod 400 secrets/auth_token
#
# The sync API is fail-closed: with no token configured, /v1 refuses to serve at all.
# DAYMARK_AUTH_TOKEN_FILE=/run/secrets/companion_auth_token
